  "narrative": {
    "intro": "Federal contract obligations in North Carolina total $3.3B for FY2026 to date. Award activity shows 130,223 actions with an average award size of $25.1K. Obligations fell 64.9% year over year. The Department of Defense is the largest buyer at $1.4B, followed by the General Services Administration at $478M and the Department of Veterans Affairs at $434M. Other top buyers include the Department of Health and Human Services at $247M and the Department of Homeland Security at $241M. Top vendor recipients are GENERAL DYNAMICS INFORMATION TECHNOLOGY, INC. at $174M, SDV OFFICE SYSTEMS LLC at $151M, and RESEARCH TRIANGLE INSTITUTE at $147M. Industry concentration is led by Computer Systems Design Services at $310M, Commercial and Institutional Building Construction at $236M, and Engineering Services at $200M. Historical fiscal-year obligations show variation: FY21 was $6.7B, FY22 was $8.6B, FY23 was $7B, FY24 was $7.2B, and FY25 was $9.3B. Current-year totals are partial because fiscal year to date reporting is incomplete.",
    "sections": [
      {
        "heading": "Why did obligations drop so much year over year?",
        "body": "Obligations fell 64.9% year over year. That decline appears alongside a current total of $3.3B for FY2026 to date versus prior fiscal-year totals such as $9.3B in FY25. The fall in obligations coincides with a high award count of 130,223 and a relatively small average award size of $25.1K. This mix suggests many small awards are contributing to the current-year total rather than a few very large awards."
      },
      {
        "heading": "Which agencies, vendors, and industries are most important now?",
        "body": "The Department of Defense is the top buying agency at $1.4B, followed by GSA at $478M and the Department of Veterans Affairs at $434M. Top vendors include GENERAL DYNAMICS INFORMATION TECHNOLOGY, INC. at $174M, SDV OFFICE SYSTEMS LLC at $151M, and RESEARCH TRIANGLE INSTITUTE at $147M. Leading industries by obligations are Computer Systems Design Services at $310M, Commercial and Institutional Building Construction at $236M, and Engineering Services at $200M."
      }
    ]
  },
